::STAT_U05_UA_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U05_UA_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DCR.1
Question:
A value calculated from a sample is called what?
{
=A. Statistic#A statistic is calculated from sample data.
~B. Parameter#A parameter describes an entire population.
~C. Population#A population is the full group, not the calculated value.
~D. Treatment#A treatment is assigned in an experiment.
}

::STAT_U05_UA_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U05_UA_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DCR.2
Question:
Why is random sampling important?
{
=A. It helps the sample represent the population#Random sampling reduces selection bias and supports representativeness.
~B. It guarantees no variation#Random samples still vary.
~C. It replaces the need for a population#The population must still be defined.
~D. It always proves causation#Causation depends on study design, not random sampling alone.
}

::STAT_U05_UA_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U05_UA_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DCR.3
Question:
A sample chosen because people are easy to reach is usually what type?
{
~A. Cluster sample#Cluster samples choose whole groups.
=B. Convenience sample#Convenience samples use easily available participants.
~C. Stratified sample#Stratified samples choose from subgroups.
~D. Census#A census includes everyone.
}

::STAT_U05_UA_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U05_UA_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.INF.1
Question:
A survey estimate is 62% with margin of error 4 percentage points. What interval is represented?
{
~A. 54% to 58%#This interval is not centered at 62%.
~B. 62% to 66%#This only adds the margin.
~C. 4% to 62%#The margin is not the lower endpoint.
=D. 58% to 66%#Subtract and add 4 percentage points.
}

::STAT_U05_UA_Q05::
Question ID: STAT_U05_UA_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.INF.2
Question:
What does confidence level describe?
{
=A. The long-run success rate of the interval method#Confidence level describes the method over repeated samples.
~B. The number of people surveyed#Sample size is not the definition of confidence level.
~C. The exact percent who agree#A sample percent is an estimate, not confidence level.
~D. The graph title#A graph title does not define confidence.
}

::STAT_U05_UA_Q06::
Question ID: STAT_U05_UA_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.INF.3
Question:
What do repeated simulated samples show?
{
~A. That every estimate is identical#Repeated samples usually differ.
~B. That no model is needed#Simulation is based on a model or process.
=C. How estimates vary#Simulation helps display sampling variability.
~D. That sampling cannot be studied#Sampling variability can be studied through simulation.
}

::STAT_U05_UA_Q07::
Question ID: STAT_U05_UA_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.INF.4
Question:
An observed result near the center of simulated results is usually what?
{
=A. Consistent with the model#A central result is typical under the model.
~B. Proof of bias#A typical result alone does not prove bias.
~C. Impossible under the model#A typical result is not impossible.
~D. A census#A census means data from everyone.
}

::STAT_U05_UA_Q08::
Question ID: STAT_U05_UA_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DCR.4
Question:
Which design assigns treatments?
{
~A. Convenience sample#Convenience sampling describes how people are selected.
~B. Survey#Surveys ask questions.
=C. Experiment#Experiments assign treatments to compare effects.
~D. Observational study#Observational studies do not assign treatment.
}

::STAT_U05_UA_Q09::
Question ID: STAT_U05_UA_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DCR.5
Question:
Why are causal claims limited in observational studies?
{
~A. They never collect data#Observational studies do collect data.
=B. Confounding variables may explain the relationship#Without random assignment, another variable may explain the association.
~C. They always assign treatments#Experiments assign treatments.
~D. They are always censuses#Observational studies do not have to include everyone.
}

::STAT_U05_UA_Q10::
Question ID: STAT_U05_UA_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.INF.5
Question:
Two random samples can support what kind of conclusion?
{
=A. Inference about two populations#Random samples support population comparisons.
~B. Treatment effect from assignment#Treatment effects require an experiment with assigned treatments.
~C. Exact population values with no uncertainty#Sample results still include uncertainty.
~D. Permutation count#Permutation is a counting method.
}

::STAT_U05_UA_Q11::
Question ID: STAT_U05_UA_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.INF.6
Question:
Random assignment in an experiment helps with what?
{
~A. Avoiding data collection#Experiments still collect outcome data.
~B. Choosing a biased sample#Random assignment is not a biased selection method.
~C. Making every result identical#Results can still vary.
=D. Fair comparison of treatments#Random assignment helps balance groups before treatment.
}

::STAT_U05_UA_Q12::
Question ID: STAT_U05_UA_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.INF.7
Question:
A difference far outside a simulation distribution is best described as what?
{
~A. A graph label#This is an interpretation, not a label.
~B. Always meaningless#Unusual results can be meaningful evidence.
~C. The same as the center#Far outside is not the same as center.
=D. Unusual under the model#Far-out results may be statistically meaningful.
}

::STAT_U05_UA_Q13::
Question ID: STAT_U05_UA_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DMC.1
Question:
What should be checked when evaluating a statistical report?
{
=A. Sampling method, graph or table, argument, and statistics#Reliable evaluation checks methods, displays, arguments, and statistics.
~B. Only the font size#Font size does not validate a claim.
~C. Only whether the headline is exciting#Exciting headlines can still be misleading.
~D. Only the color of the chart#Color alone does not determine statistical quality.
}

